% ============================================================================
% bill-stage-successor — which stage a bill moves to next on its way
% through Congress (adj-facts-stdlib, CIVICS).
% ============================================================================
% "How does a bill become a law?" is the canonical elementary civics
% question, and this table answers it one honest hop at a time. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? bill_stage_successor(committee_review, $N)
%       % first_chamber_vote
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `bill_stage_successor(stage, next_stage)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the next stage WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls. The query also runs BACKWARD, which answers "what has to
% happen before this?":
%
%     ? bill_stage_successor($P, presented_to_president)
%       % vote_on_same_version
%
% WHY A SUCCESSOR RELATION AND NOT A STEP NUMBER. This stdlib already
% ships four ordinal-position tables — `astronomy/moon-phases.adj`'s
% `moon_phase_order`, `astronomy/planets.adj`'s `planet_order`,
% `biology/mitosis-phase-order.adj`, and
% `earth-science/sedimentary-rock-formation-step.adj` — and the obvious
% move here was a fifth: `bill_stage_step(stage, step_number)`, 1 through
% 7. That would have been WRONG for this source, and the reason is worth
% stating because the failure would have been invisible.
%
% Those four tables all decode sources that state POSITIONS: a numbered or
% explicitly-counted sequence. This source does not. It states
% TRANSITIONS, in continuous narrative prose, each marked by its own
% connective — "Once a bill is introduced, it is assigned to a
% committee...", "The bill is THEN put before that chamber...", "IF the
% bill passes one body of Congress, IT GOES TO the other body...". Every
% sentence says what comes AFTER what. None says what number anything is.
%
% Assigning absolute ordinals would have required inventing an answer to a
% question the page never addresses: where does the count start? Is
% `introduced` step 1, or is the idea-origin list before it step 1? Pick
% differently and EVERY number changes, yet no test would fail and no
% citation would look wrong — the table would simply assert positions its
% own source never states. A successor relation records exactly the
% adjacency the prose does state, nothing more, and has the incidental
% virtue of being stable: if a future source names an additional stage,
% two rows change here, whereas an ordinal table would renumber wholesale.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from USA.gov's "How laws are made" page,
% "How a bill becomes a law" section. Each row decodes exactly one of the
% prose's own transition sentences:
%
%     stage -> next_stage                                    transition sentence (verbatim)
%     ----------------------------------------------------   -------------------------------------------------------
%     introduced -> committee_review                          "Once a bill is introduced, it is assigned to a
%                                                                committee whose members will research, discuss, and
%                                                                make changes to the bill."
%     committee_review -> first_chamber_vote                  "The bill is then put before that chamber to be voted
%                                                                on."
%     first_chamber_vote -> second_chamber_process            "If the bill passes one body of Congress, it goes to
%                                                                the other body to go through a similar process of
%                                                                research, discussion, changes, and voting."
%     second_chamber_process -> reconcile_differences         "Once both bodies vote to accept a bill, they must work
%                                                                out any differences between the two versions."
%     reconcile_differences -> vote_on_same_version           "Then both chambers vote on the same version of the
%                                                                bill."
%     vote_on_same_version -> presented_to_president          "If it passes, they present it to the president."
%     presented_to_president -> president_considers           "The president then considers the bill."
%
% curl-fetched the raw page and read it byte-for-byte before writing this
% file (not just an AI-summarized WebFetch). `trust authoritative` —
% USA.gov is the U.S. General Services Administration's official guide to
% government information, a first-party .gov publisher of exactly the fact
% being recalled, the same tier and the SAME page
% `chamber-exclusive-power.adj` and `veto-override.adj` already cite.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - `president_considers` HAS no successor row, and that is deliberate
%     rather than an oversight. From that point the prose BRANCHES: the
%     president "can approve the bill and sign it into law", or can "refuse
%     to approve a bill" (a veto), or may let it go unsigned into a pocket
%     veto. A successor relation cannot honestly represent a branch — it
%     would have to pick one outcome arbitrarily, or return three
%     "next stages" as if they all happened. The chain this table records
%     is the LINEAR portion of the journey, and it stops exactly where the
%     source stops being linear. What happens on each branch is already
%     held by `checks-and-balances.adj` (the veto as an act) and
%     `veto-override.adj` (whether that act can be undone).
%   - The idea-origin list the same page states — "The idea for a bill can
%     come from a: Sitting member of the U.S. Senate or House of
%     Representatives / Proposal during a congressional candidate's
%     election campaign / Petition by people or citizen groups..." —
%     answers WHERE a bill starts, not what stage follows what. Different
%     axis, its own future table.
%   - Any stage not named by one of the seven transition sentences above is
%     deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS rather than inventing a
%     step in the process.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table bill_stage_successor {
    columns stage, next_stage

    row (introduced, committee_review)
    row (committee_review, first_chamber_vote)
    row (first_chamber_vote, second_chamber_process)
    row (second_chamber_process, reconcile_differences)
    row (reconcile_differences, vote_on_same_version)
    row (vote_on_same_version, presented_to_president)
    row (presented_to_president, president_considers)

    source "Once a bill is introduced, it is assigned to a committee whose members will research, discuss, and make changes to the bill."
    locator "https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made"
    trust authoritative

    cites "The bill is then put before that chamber to be voted on." locator "https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made"
    cites "If the bill passes one body of Congress, it goes to the other body to go through a similar process of research, discussion, changes, and voting." locator "https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made"
    cites "Once both bodies vote to accept a bill, they must work out any differences between the two versions." locator "https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made"
    cites "Then both chambers vote on the same version of the bill." locator "https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made"
    cites "If it passes, they present it to the president." locator "https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made"
    cites "The president then considers the bill." locator "https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made"
}
